'''Híbito''' (Xibitoana) is a poorly attested, extinct language formerly spoken by the Híbito people along the Bombanaje, Jelache, and Huayabamba rivers, tributaries of the [[Huallaga River]] in northern Peru. The last speaker, Natividad Grández del Castillo, died between 1996 and 2000.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Alexander-Bakkerus |first=Astrid |url=https://scholarlypublications.universiteitleiden.nl/access/item%3A2887508/view |title=Eighteenth-century Cholón |date=2005 |publisher=LOT |isbn=978-90-76864-86-0 |series=LOT |location=Utrecht}}</ref><ref name="Loukotka">{{cite book |last=Loukotka |first=Čestmír |authorlink=Čestmír Loukotka |title=Classification of South American Indian languages |url=https://archive.org/details/classificationof0007louk |url-access=registration |publisher=UCLA Latin American Center |year=1968 |location=Los Angeles}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|access-date=6 November 2024|url=http://multitree.org/codes/hib.html|title=Híbito|url-status=dead|archive-date=26 September 2012|publisher=[[Linguist List|LINGUIST List]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120926150901/http://multitree.org/codes/hib.html}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Citation |last=Alexander-Bakkerus |first=Astrid |title=Híbito and Cholón |date=2024-12-31 |work=The Oxford Guide to the Languages of the Central Andes |pages=392–418 |editor-last=Urban |editor-first=Matthias |url=https://academic.oup.com/book/59575/chapter/503209455 |access-date=2026-02-26 |edition=1 |publisher=Oxford University PressOxford |language=en |doi=10.1093/oso/9780198849926.003.0013 |isbn=978-0-19-884992-6|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== Documentation ==
The first known documentation of Híbito was made in 1676 by the [[Franciscans|Franciscan]] missionary Joseph de Araujo, who is said to have written a grammar, vocabulary and [[catechism]] in the language; however, these documents have not been found.<ref name=":0" />

== Vocabulary ==

=== The "plan" of Martínez Compañón ===
Hibito is attested in a 43-word list in a document referred to as the "plan" collected by [[Bishop of Trujillo]] [[Baltasar Jaime Martínez Compañón]] between 1782 and 1785. The "plan" is part of a larger work, known as the [[Codex Martínez Compañón]], detailing life in colonial Peru.<ref name="Martinez">Martínez Compañón, Baltasar Jaime. 1985 [1782-1790]. ''Trujillo del Perú en el siglo XVIII'', vol. 2. Madrid: Ediciones Cultura Hispánica.</ref> Notably, the work also contains a number of watercolors, which were captioned by Martínez Compañón's personal secretary [[:es:Pedro Agustín de Echevarri|Pedro Agustín de Echevarri]], who presumably also wrote down the "plan".

There are two copies of the "plan", one held in [[Bogotá]] and the other in [[Madrid]]. Both copies include 43-word lists for the [[Quechuan languages|Quechua]], [[Mochica language|Mochica]] (Yunga), [[Sechura language|Sechura]], [[Colán dialect|Colán]], [[Catacao dialect|Catacaos]], [[Culle language|Culli]], Hibito and [[Cholón language|Cholón]] languages, as well as Spanish. The Colán and Catacaos languages are generally subsumed under the name ''Tallán'', and they are closely related, probably dialects of a single language. The two versions of the "plan" have certain differences from each other, particularly in the spelling of the transcriptions.

A number of diacritics are employed in the vocabularies. Their meaning is not elaborated upon in the "plan", although certain diacritics are employed in only some of the languages, and are apparently not merely decorative in purpose.<ref name=":02">{{Cite book |last=Urban |first=Matthias |url=https://core.ac.uk/download/pdf/304709916.pdf |title=Lost languages of the Peruvian north coast |date=2019 |publisher=Gebr. Mann Verlag |isbn=978-3-7861-2826-7 |series=Estudios Indiana |location=Berlin |pages=73–96 |chapter=Mochica |oclc=on1090545680}}</ref>

